## What the code does

Three `_set` methods and one `_copy` method in calendars-client validate "no creation id is empty" using an explicit `for` loop with an early-return inside:

```rust
// crate-jmap-calendars-client/src/methods/calendar.rs:96-104
if let Some(ref m) = create {
    for k in m.keys() {
        if k.is_empty() {
            return Err(jmap_base_client::ClientError::InvalidArgument(
                "calendar_set: create map key (creation id) may not be empty".into(),
            ));
        }
    }
}
```

Same shape in event.rs:105-113, event_copy.rs:28-34, participant_identity.rs:79-88.

## What idiomatic Rust would do

The loop body is a pure predicate ("is there any empty key") with one action on a hit. The Rust-idiomatic spelling is `Iterator::any`:

```rust
if let Some(m) = &create {
    if m.keys().any(|k| k.is_empty()) {
        return Err(jmap_base_client::ClientError::InvalidArgument(
            "calendar_set: create map key (creation id) may not be empty".into(),
        ));
    }
}
```

(Note the parallel finding that `Some(ref m) =` is itself a stale pre-2018 pattern, filed separately. Both fixes compose into the same site.)

## Why it matters

`Iterator::any` communicates intent ("is there any element matching this predicate") at a glance; the `for` loop with embedded `if` and `return` makes the reader trace the control flow to recover the same semantics. Three nesting levels (`if let` → `for` → `if`) collapses to two (`if let` → `if`).

This is a low-stakes idiom — not a correctness bug, not a performance issue (both compile to the same code under any optimisation level worth caring about). The engineering argument is reader comprehension: a serious Rust practitioner expects `Iterator::any` for "does any element match" and reads `for ... if ... return` as "this code was written by someone who hasn't internalised the iterator combinators yet".

The pattern is duplicated four times in this crate. Fixing one teaches the reader nothing; fixing all four restores the cookie-cutter consistency that workspace AGENTS.md treats as a first-order goal.

## Why not just leave it

Pure-style change with no behavioural difference is exactly the territory the bikeshedding warning in the slash-command prompt is meant to constrain. The engineering basis here is:

1. The same defensive guard is at four call sites and they are about to be propagated to (potentially) every other extension-client sibling under any future canonical-template sweep. Settle on the idiomatic shape before the pattern spreads to six more crates.

2. The canonical mail-client does NOT have this guard at all (it doesn't validate empty creation ids). If/when the workspace decides this validation should be canonical, sweeping the cleaner shape is meaningfully easier than sweeping the `for`-with-embedded-return shape.

## Fix shape

Mechanical `for k in m.keys() { if k.is_empty() { ...err... } }` → `if m.keys().any(|k| k.is_empty()) { ...err... }` at four sites.

